A complete diagnostic pass across all four phases finds no fault in the live network. The user's report claims that 200.1.1.0/24 has "vanished from the routers' address book" and that pc1 (195.11.14.2) cannot reach pc2 (200.1.1.2), but every layer of evidence contradicts this.

**Affected component:** None — the network is operating normally.

**Mechanism (why the ticket is not confirmed):** The BGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is in the Established state on both peers. router1 is correctly advertising 195.11.14.0/24 and router2 is correctly advertising 200.1.1.0/24. There are no null/blackhole routes, no ACL blocking BGP or ICMP, no tc shaping, no duplicate MACs, and no addressing misconfiguration on either host or router. A live end-to-end ping from pc1 to 200.1.1.2 completes with 0% loss (RTT ≈ 0.93 ms), and the reverse path is equally clean.

**Localization:** No specific device or interface is implicated. Phase A (infra_sweep, l2_snapshot), Phase B (bgp_snapshot, tc_snapshot), and Phase D (pressure_sweep) all returned zero flags across all four devices.

**Root cause:** There is no current fault — the BGP session is established, routes are propagated correctly, and end-to-end reachability is confirmed at 0% packet loss.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The network is healthy as observed; the helpdesk report does not correspond to any live anomaly detectable in the current state.
